      <section class="tg-section" id="question">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Question</h2>

        <p>Near the end of Mark's Gospel, the risen Jesus seems to list the signs that will follow
          those who believe: driving out demons, speaking in new tongues, handling snakes, drinking
          poison unharmed, and healing the sick
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 16:17-18">Mark 16:17–18</a>). Many Pentecostal and charismatic
          Christians treat these signs, and tongues above all, as the proof that a person has received
          the Holy Spirit. Many other believers, who have never spoken in tongues, have been left
          wondering whether something is missing in them.</p>

        <p>This study asks four questions. Do these verses belong to Mark? What does the rest of the
          New Testament say about tongues and healing? Does Scripture make any single sign the proof of
          the Spirit's presence? And what, according to the New Testament, are the marks of a true
          believer?</p>

        <p>It tries to treat charismatic Christians as the brothers and sisters they are, and to treat
          their claims as Scripture itself asks: "test all things. Hold fast to what is good"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:21">1 Thessalonians 5:21</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Translation</span>
          Scripture quotations on this page are from the Berean Standard Bible (BSB).
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="mark-16">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What Mark 16:17–18 Says, and the Question About Its Place in Mark</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Text</h3>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Whoever believes and is baptized will be saved, but whoever does not believe will be
          condemned. And these signs will accompany those who believe: In My name they will drive out
          demons; they will speak in new tongues; they will pick up snakes with their hands, and if
          they drink any deadly poison, it will not harm them; they will lay their hands on the sick,
          and they will be made well.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 16:16-18">Mark 16:16–18</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Three observations are worth making before anything else.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Salvation rests on belief, not on signs.</strong> The verse before the list says
            what saves: "Whoever believes and is baptized will be saved." The signs "accompany"
            belief. They are not added to it.</li>
          <li><strong>The list is a package of five.</strong> If this passage were a test for each
            individual believer, it would require demons driven out, new tongues, snakes handled,
            poison drunk, and the sick healed. Churches that use the verse as a membership test almost
            always keep tongues and healing and quietly set aside the snakes and the poison. A few
            small churches have taken all five literally, with tragic results. Either the passage is a
            test of every believer, or it is not. It cannot be a test only for the signs we find
            comfortable.</li>
          <li><strong>The signs have a purpose.</strong> The passage ends: "the Lord worked through
            them, confirming His word by the signs that accompanied it"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 16:20">Mark 16:20</a>). The signs confirmed the message the
            apostles preached. They were not a badge worn by every convert.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Textual Question</h3>

        <p>Every honest reader of Mark 16 has to face a fact: verses 9–20 are missing from some of our
          oldest manuscripts. The two great fourth-century Greek Bibles, Codex Sinaiticus and Codex
          Vaticanus, end Mark at verse 8: "And in their fear they did not say a word to anyone"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 16:8">Mark 16:8</a>). Eusebius and Jerome, writing in the
          fourth and early fifth centuries, said that the accurate Greek copies they knew lacked the
          passage. Other manuscripts carry a short alternative ending, and the great majority of later
          manuscripts carry the long ending we have in our Bibles. The style and vocabulary of verses
          9–20 also differ noticeably from the rest of Mark.</p>

        <p>On the other side, the long ending is early. Irenaeus, in the late second century, appears
          to quote it, and it was widely read in the church for most of its history. Faithful scholars
          who hold the Bible to be God's Word have come to different conclusions: some receive these
          verses as Scripture the church has always read, and others regard them as a very early
          addition. Most modern Bibles set them apart with a note.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Sound Conclusion</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">A doctrine as weighty as "who is truly a Christian" should not
            rest on a passage whose place in Mark is uncertain, especially when the New Testament
            teaches the matter plainly elsewhere.</p>
        </div>

        <p>You do not need to settle that debate here to draw that conclusion. And these signs do
          appear elsewhere. Demons were driven out and the sick healed
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 6:13">Mark 6:13</a>), tongues were spoken
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:4">Acts 2:4</a>), and Paul was bitten by a viper and
          suffered "no ill effects" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 28:3-6">Acts 28:3–6</a>). The real
          question is not whether such things happened. It is what they were for, and whether they
          prove that a person belongs to Christ.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="gifts">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Tongues and Gifts in the Rest of the New Testament</h2>

        <p>The New Testament speaks of tongues in two places that matter most: the book of Acts, which
          tells what happened, and Paul's first letter to the Corinthians, which teaches how the gift
          is to be understood and used.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">In Acts: The Gospel Crosses Boundaries</h3>

        <p>Tongues appear in Acts at moments when the gospel reached a new group of people.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Jerusalem (Acts 2).</strong> At Pentecost the disciples "were all filled with the
            Holy Spirit and began to speak in other tongues as the Spirit enabled them"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:4">Acts 2:4</a>). The crowd of visitors from many nations
            heard the wonders of God in their own languages
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:5-11">Acts 2:5–11</a>). Peter explains the promise:
            "Repent and be baptized, every one of you, in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness
            of your sins, and you will receive the gift of the Holy Spirit. This promise belongs to you
            and your children and to all who are far off"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:38-39">Acts 2:38–39</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Samaria (Acts 8).</strong> The Samaritans believed and were baptized, but "the
            Holy Spirit had not yet fallen upon any of them." Then Peter and John laid their hands on
            them, "and they received the Holy Spirit"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 8:16-17">Acts 8:16–17</a>). The text does not say that they
            spoke in tongues.</li>
          <li><strong>The Gentiles (Acts 10).</strong> "The Holy Spirit fell upon all who heard his
            message," and the Jewish believers "heard them speaking in tongues and exalting God." Peter
            drew the conclusion: "They have received the Holy Spirit just as we have!"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 10:44-47">Acts 10:44–47</a>). Tongues were the visible proof
            to skeptical Jewish Christians that God had accepted the Gentiles.</li>
          <li><strong>Ephesus (Acts 19).</strong> When Paul laid his hands on a small group of
            disciples who had known only John's baptism, "the Holy Spirit came upon them, and they
            spoke in tongues and prophesied" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 19:6">Acts 19:6</a>).</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Putting these together, the pattern is not one of ordinary conversions. It is a pattern of
          the gospel advancing from Jews, to Samaritans, to Gentiles, to the disciples of John, with
          God publicly confirming each step. Notice also what is <em>absent</em>. Acts reports many
          other conversions, including the Ethiopian official (Acts 8), Lydia, and the Philippian
          jailer (Acts 16), and it does not mention tongues in them. The order of repentance, baptism,
          and the Spirit's coming also varies from one account to another.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">In 1 Corinthians: The Gift in Its Proper Place</h3>

        <p>Paul wrote <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:1-14:40">1 Corinthians 12–14</a> to
          correct a church that prized spectacular gifts and neglected love and order. Four of his
          points stand out.</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The gifts are many, and God distributes them.</strong> "There are different
            gifts, but the same Spirit." The list includes wisdom, knowledge, faith, "gifts of
            healing," miracles, prophecy, discernment, "various tongues," and the interpretation of
            tongues. "All these are the work of one and the same Spirit, who apportions them to each
            one as He determines" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:4-11">1 Corinthians 12:4–11</a>).
            The gifts are given "for the common good"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:7">12:7</a>), not for private status.</li>
          <li><strong>Love outranks every gift.</strong> "If I speak in the tongues of men and of
            angels, but have not love, I am only a ringing gong or a clanging cymbal"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:1">1 Corinthians 13:1</a>). A person can have
            the most dramatic gift and still be spiritually bankrupt.</li>
          <li><strong>Tongues build up the speaker, and prophecy builds up the church.</strong> "He who
            speaks in a tongue does not speak to men, but to God. Indeed, no one understands him; he
            utters mysteries in the Spirit." "The one who speaks in a tongue edifies himself, but the
            one who prophesies edifies the church"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:2">1 Corinthians 14:2</a>,
            <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:4">4</a>). Paul says, "I thank God that I speak
            in tongues more than all of you," and in the next breath, "in the church, I would rather
            speak five coherent words to instruct others than ten thousand words in a tongue"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:18-19">14:18–19</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The gift must be orderly.</strong> In the assembly, "two, or at most three,
            should speak in turn, and someone must interpret. But if there is no interpreter, he
            should remain silent in the church and speak only to himself and God"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:27-28">1 Corinthians 14:27–28</a>). Paul's
            summary is balanced: "be eager to prophesy, and do not forbid speaking in tongues. But
            everything must be done in a proper and orderly manner"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:39-40">14:39–40</a>).</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Paul neither bans the gift nor exalts it. He puts it in its place. Whatever one concludes
          about tongues today, any use of the gift should look like what he describes.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="proof">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Does Scripture Make Tongues the Proof of the Spirit?</h2>

        <p>The New Testament gives several reasons to answer no, if the question is whether tongues, or
          any one sign, is the proof that a person has the Spirit or belongs to Christ.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Paul Expects the Answer "No"</h3>

        <p>After listing the gifts in the church, Paul asks: "Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are
          all teachers? Do all work miracles? Do all have gifts of healing? Do all speak in tongues? Do
          all interpret?" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:29-30">1 Corinthians 12:29–30</a>).
          The way the questions are put expects the answer no to each one. Not all speak in tongues,
          and not all have gifts of healing, yet Paul addresses the whole church as "the body of
          Christ" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:27">12:27</a>). The Spirit is given to
          every believer, as Paul said a few verses earlier: "in one Spirit we were all baptized into
          one body, whether Jews or Greeks, slave or free, and we were all given one Spirit to drink"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:13">12:13</a>). Every believer has the Spirit. Not
          every believer has the same gift.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Scripture Says How We Know We Have the Spirit</h3>

        <p>The New Testament answers the question directly, and tongues are not the answer.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Confession of Jesus as Lord.</strong> "No one can say, 'Jesus is Lord,' except by
            the Holy Spirit" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:3">1 Corinthians 12:3</a>). Paul
            gives this test right after reminding the Corinthians of their pagan past, when they had
            been "influenced and led astray to mute idols"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:2">12:2</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Faith in the gospel.</strong> "Did you receive the Spirit by works of the law, or
            by hearing with faith?" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 3:2">Galatians 3:2</a>). Paul
            tells believers that in Christ, "having heard and believed the word of truth—the gospel of
            your salvation—you were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:13">Ephesians 1:13</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The Spirit's own witness.</strong> "All who are led by the Spirit of God are sons
            of God. For you did not receive a spirit of slavery that returns you to fear, but you
            received the Spirit of sonship, by whom we cry, 'Abba! Father!' The Spirit Himself
            testifies with our spirit that we are God's children"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:14-16">Romans 8:14–16</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Belonging to Christ.</strong> "If anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, he
            does not belong to Christ" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:9">Romans 8:9</a>). That is
            the heart of the matter: the Spirit marks those who belong to Christ, and Scripture
            describes what that looks like in many places besides Mark 16.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Corinth Had Every Gift, and Was Still Immature</h3>

        <p>The Corinthian church shows that gifts and holiness are not the same thing. Paul thanks God
          that "you do not lack any spiritual gift"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 1:7">1 Corinthians 1:7</a>), and a few chapters later
          he writes: "Brothers, I could not address you as spiritual, but as worldly—as infants in
          Christ . . . since there is jealousy and dissension among you, are you not worldly?"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:1">1 Corinthians 3:1</a>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 3:3">3</a>). A church can be spectacularly gifted and
          spiritually immature at the same moment. That is why Paul placed chapter 13 in the middle of
          his teaching on gifts.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Jesus on Seeking Signs</h3>

        <p>Jesus was not impressed by the demand for signs. "A wicked and adulterous generation demands
          a sign" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 12:39">Matthew 12:39</a>). When the seventy-two
          returned thrilled that "even the demons submit to us in Your name," He answered, "do not
          rejoice that the spirits submit to you, but rejoice that your names are written in heaven"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 10:17">Luke 10:17</a>,
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 10:20">20</a>). Even a believer's power over demons was not to
          be the ground of his joy or his assurance. Salvation was.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What the Signs Were For</h3>

        <p>The New Testament says plainly what signs did. They confirmed the message and the
          messengers. The gospel "was first announced by the Lord, was confirmed to us by those who
          heard Him, and was affirmed by God through signs, wonders, various miracles, and gifts of the
          Holy Spirit distributed according to His will"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 2:3-4">Hebrews 2:3–4</a>). Paul could say, "The true marks
          of an apostle—signs, wonders, and miracles—were performed among you"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 12:12">2 Corinthians 12:12</a>). At Iconium, the Lord
          "affirmed the message of His grace by enabling them to perform signs and wonders"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 14:3">Acts 14:3</a>). Signs authenticate a message and its
          messengers. Nowhere does the New Testament say that each convert must produce them to prove
          that he is converted.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Cost of a Required Sign</h3>

        <p>When one experience is made the proof of membership, two harms follow. Sincere believers who
          have never had it are told, directly or by implication, that they are second-class or even
          unsaved, which shifts their assurance from Christ's finished work to their own spiritual
          experience. And those who have had the experience may come to rest on it, rather than on
          Christ.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="differ">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Where Christians Differ</h2>

        <p>This is a question on which sincere, Bible-believing Christians disagree, so it is worth
          stating each position as its holders would state it.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Pentecostal View: Tongues as Initial Evidence</h3>

        <p>Classical Pentecostal churches teach that after conversion a believer may receive a "baptism
          in the Holy Spirit," an empowering for witness and service, and that speaking in tongues is
          the initial physical evidence of it. They rest this on the pattern in Acts 2, 10, and 19, and
          on Paul's own words, "I wish that all of you could speak in tongues"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:5">1 Corinthians 14:5</a>). They point out that
          Paul commanded, "do not forbid speaking in tongues"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:39">14:39</a>), and they accuse much of the church
          of being so afraid of excess that it has quenched the Spirit (compare
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:19">1 Thessalonians 5:19</a>). They also deserve
          credit for something the wider church has often lacked: a confident expectation that God is
          present and acts in answer to prayer.</p>

        <p>Most Pentecostals teach that salvation comes by faith in Christ alone, and not by tongues.
          The broader charismatic movement is more varied still, and many charismatics do not make
          tongues a requirement. The concern raised in this study is about what the emphasis can do in
          practice, when a believer who has never spoken in tongues is made to feel spiritually
          incomplete.</p>

        <p>Others reply that Acts is a narrative that tells what happened, while the letters teach
          doctrine, and that the narratives themselves are not uniform. The Spirit comes before baptism
          in Acts 10, after it in Acts 8, and with the laying on of hands in Acts 19. They add that
          Paul describes the Spirit's baptism as something <em>all</em> believers share
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:13">1 Corinthians 12:13</a>) and asks, "Do all
          speak in tongues?" expecting the answer no.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Continuationist View: The Gifts Continue, Without a Test</h3>

        <p>Continuationists hold that all the gifts of the Spirit, including prophecy, healing, and
          tongues, remain available to the church, and they encourage prayer for the sick, as James
          does: "He should call the elders of the church to pray over him and anoint him with oil in
          the name of the Lord. And the prayer offered in faith will restore the one who is sick"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:14-15">James 5:14–15</a>). But they refuse to turn any gift
          into a requirement. Tongues are one gift among many, given "as He determines"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:11">1 Corinthians 12:11</a>), and every gift must
          be tested by Scripture and exercised in order and love.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Cessationist View: The Sign Gifts Belonged to the Apostolic Age</h3>

        <p>Cessationists hold that the miraculous sign gifts, such as tongues, prophecy, and apostolic
          healing, served to confirm the apostles' message while the New Testament was being given, and
          then ceased. They point to the language that ties signs to the apostles and their circle:
          "The true marks of an apostle—signs, wonders, and miracles"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 12:12">2 Corinthians 12:12</a>), the church "built on
          the foundation of the apostles and prophets"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:20">Ephesians 2:20</a>), and the message "confirmed to
          us by those who heard Him" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 2:3">Hebrews 2:3</a>). They read
          Paul's words, "where there are tongues, they will be restrained"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:8">1 Corinthians 13:8</a>), together with "when
          the perfect comes" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:10">13:10</a>), as pointing to
          the completion of Scripture. Continuationists respond that "the perfect" better fits the
          return of Christ, since Paul goes on, "then we shall see face to face"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:12">13:12</a>), and that nothing in the New
          Testament says the gifts would stop with the apostles. Cessationists do not deny that God
          heals in answer to prayer today. They deny that the apostolic sign gifts continue as a normal
          part of church life.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What All Three Can Agree On</h3>

        <p>Thoughtful voices in all three camps agree on far more than the arguments suggest. Salvation
          is by grace through faith in Christ. Every believer is indwelt by the Spirit. Love is the
          greatest sign. Any claim to a spiritual experience must be tested by the Scriptures. And no
          one should despise a brother or sister who sees the matter differently. Wherever you land on
          the gifts, the marks of a true believer, which we turn to now, are the same.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="warning">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Warning from Jesus: Signs Do Not Prove a Person</h2>

        <p>The most sobering passage on this topic comes from Jesus Himself, at the end of the Sermon on
          the Mount.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">"I Never Knew You"</h3>

        <p>"Beware of false prophets. They come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are
          ravenous wolves. By their fruit you will recognize them." Then, after describing the good
          tree and the bad tree, He says: "Not everyone who says to Me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the
          kingdom of heaven, but only he who does the will of My Father in heaven. Many will say to Me
          on that day, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in Your name, and in Your name drive out
          demons and perform many miracles?' Then I will tell them plainly, 'I never knew you; depart
          from Me, you workers of lawlessness!'"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:15-23">Matthew 7:15–23</a>).</p>

        <p>Read that slowly. The people Jesus rejects point to prophecy, exorcism, and miracles, in His
          name. They list signs very much like those in Mark 16. Jesus does not say that their works
          were fake. He says that works are not the test. The test is whether they did "the will of My
          Father" and whether their lives bore good fruit. Power without obedience does not prove that
          a person belongs to Christ. Obedience flowing from a relationship does.</p>

        <p>This does not mean that every miracle claim today is false. It means that no miracle,
          however real, can serve as the proof of a person's standing with God.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Signs Can Be Real and Still Mislead</h3>

        <p>The Old Testament states the principle first. "If a prophet or dreamer of dreams arises among
          you and proclaims a sign or wonder to you, and if the sign or wonder he has spoken to you
          comes about, but he says, 'Let us follow other gods . . . and let us worship them,' you must
          not listen to the words of that prophet or dreamer. For the LORD your God is testing you to
          find out whether you love Him with all your heart and with all your soul"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 13:1-3">Deuteronomy 13:1–3</a>). A sign that comes true
          does not validate a message that contradicts God's revealed truth. The message tests the sign,
          and not the reverse.</p>

        <p>The New Testament warns the same way. "False Christs and false prophets will appear and
          perform great signs and wonders that would deceive even the elect, if that were possible"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 24:24">Matthew 24:24</a>). The coming of the lawless one
          "will be accompanied by the working of Satan, with every kind of power, sign, and false
          wonder" (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:9">2 Thessalonians 2:9</a>). Supernatural
          power in itself has never been sufficient evidence that God is the source.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Tests the New Testament Gives</h3>

        <p>John gives the church a simple, durable test: "Beloved, do not believe every spirit, but test
          the spirits to see whether they are from God. For many false prophets have gone out into the
          world. By this you will know the Spirit of God: Every spirit that confesses that Jesus Christ
          has come in the flesh is from God" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:1-2">1 John 4:1–2</a>).
          Paul balances the same two commands in one sentence: "Do not extinguish the Spirit. Do not
          treat prophecies with contempt, but test all things. Hold fast to what is good"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Thessalonians 5:19-21">1 Thessalonians 5:19–21</a>). Do not quench
          the Spirit, and do not stop testing.</p>

        <p>Paul also warned of people "having a form of godliness but denying its power"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Timothy 3:5">2 Timothy 3:5</a>). That warning cuts in every
          direction. It applies to a church that has correct doctrine and no life, and it applies to a
          church that has lively experiences and no truth. The aim is a church with both: sound
          teaching about Christ, and a changed life that comes from His Spirit.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="marks">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Marks of a True Believer</h2>

        <p>If signs are not the proof, what is? The New Testament answers consistently. These marks are
          not a ladder to climb in order to earn salvation. They are the evidence that the Spirit of
          God is already at work in a person.</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Faith in Jesus Christ, and confession of Him as Lord.</strong> "If you confess
            with your mouth, 'Jesus is Lord,' and believe in your heart that God raised Him from the
            dead, you will be saved" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 10:9">Romans 10:9</a>). "Everyone
            who believes that Jesus is the Christ has been born of God"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:1">1 John 5:1</a>). This is the foundation, and
            everything else grows from it.</li>
          <li><strong>The Spirit's witness and leading.</strong> "All who are led by the Spirit of God
            are sons of God," and "The Spirit Himself testifies with our spirit that we are God's
            children" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:14">Romans 8:14</a>,
            <a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:16">16</a>). A true believer has an inner assurance of
            belonging to the Father, a settled "Abba! Father!" that no one else could give.</li>
          <li><strong>The fruit of the Spirit.</strong> "The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace,
            patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:22-23">Galatians 5:22–23</a>). Paul sets this against
            "the acts of the flesh," which include "hatred, discord, jealousy, and rage; rivalries,
            divisions, factions" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:19-21">5:19–21</a>), and says,
            "Those who belong to Christ Jesus have crucified the flesh with its passions and desires"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:24">5:24</a>). Jesus said, "This is to My Father's
            glory, that you bear much fruit, proving yourselves to be My disciples"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 15:8">John 15:8</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Love for other believers.</strong> "By this everyone will know that you are My
            disciples, if you love one another" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 13:35">John 13:35</a>).
            "We know that we have passed from death to life, because we love our brothers. The one who
            does not love remains in death" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:14">1 John 3:14</a>).
            "Whoever does not love does not know God, because God is love"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:8">1 John 4:8</a>). The mark Jesus chose as the world's
            way of recognizing His disciples was love, and not a gift.</li>
          <li><strong>Obedience to Christ.</strong> "If you love Me, you will keep My commandments"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 14:15">John 14:15</a>). "By this we can be sure that we have
            come to know Him: if we keep His commandments. If anyone says, 'I know Him,' but does not
            keep His commandments, he is a liar, and the truth is not in him"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:3-4">1 John 2:3–4</a>). James makes the same point about
            faith: "Show me your faith without deeds, and I will show you my faith by my deeds"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:18">James 2:18</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Sound doctrine about Christ, and a hearing ear.</strong> "Every spirit that
            confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is from God"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:2">1 John 4:2</a>). Jesus said, "My sheep listen to My
            voice; I know them, and they follow Me" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:27">John 10:27</a>).
            A true believer wants to hear, believe, and keep the Word of God.</li>
          <li><strong>Perseverance.</strong> "If you continue in My word, you are truly My disciples"
            (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 8:31">John 8:31</a>). The believer does not endure in his own
            strength alone: "He who began a good work in you will carry it on to completion until the
            day of Christ Jesus" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 1:6">Philippians 1:6</a>).</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">These Marks Do Not Require Perfection</h3>

        <p>A true believer still sins. What marks him is how he responds: "If we confess our sins, He is
          faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:9">1 John 1:9</a>). He grieves over his failures, comes
          back to Christ, and keeps walking. Nor are these marks the cause of his salvation. "He saved
          us, not by the righteous deeds we had done, but according to His mercy, through the washing of
          new birth and renewal by the Holy Spirit" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 3:5">Titus 3:5</a>).
          They are what a saved life looks like.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">What This Means for the Modern Believer</h3>

        <p>The pattern is ordinary. A modern believer is one who trusts Christ alone for salvation,
          confesses Him as Lord, loves other Christians, obeys the Word of God, grows in the fruit of
          the Spirit, holds to the truth about Christ, and keeps going. Such a person may have an
          unusual gift or no unusual gift at all. He is, in either case, a true believer.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="tg-section" id="practice">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Putting It into Practice</h2>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">If You Have Never Spoken in Tongues</h3>

        <p>You are not a second-class Christian. Paul's question, "Do all speak in tongues?"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:30">1 Corinthians 12:30</a>), expects the answer
          no, and God "apportions them to each one as He determines"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 12:11">12:11</a>). Your assurance rests on Christ and
          on the Spirit's witness in your heart: "For it is by grace you have been saved through faith,
          and this not from yourselves; it is the gift of God, not by works"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 2:8-9">Ephesians 2:8–9</a>). Examine yourself by the
          marks in the last section, and if they are present, rest.</p>

        <p>There is nothing wrong with desiring spiritual gifts. Paul says, "Earnestly pursue love and
          eagerly desire spiritual gifts" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:1">1 Corinthians 14:1</a>),
          and Jesus promised, "how much more will your Father in heaven give the Holy Spirit to those
          who ask Him!" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 11:13">Luke 11:13</a>). Ask, then, and leave the
          answer to God. But be wary of any teacher or method that pressures you to produce a sign, that
          coaches you to imitate one, or that suggests you are not yet saved without it.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">If You Have Spoken in Tongues or Seen Healing</h3>

        <p>Give thanks, and keep the gift where Scripture puts it. Let it serve others and not your
          pride. "If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I am only a
          ringing gong or a clanging cymbal"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 13:1">1 Corinthians 13:1</a>). Honor the order Paul
          asks for in the assembly. Do not build your assurance on an experience, however vivid. Build
          it on Christ, and measure your life by the fruit of the Spirit. And do not look down on those
          who have not had your experience. They share the same Spirit.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">How to Treat Charismatic Brothers and Sisters</h3>

        <p>If you do not share their experience, resist the urge to dismiss it. Many charismatic
          Christians love the Lord, honor His Word, and live lives of real devotion. "Accept him whose
          faith is weak, without passing judgment on his opinions"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 14:1">Romans 14:1</a>) was written about other disputed
          matters, but the spirit of it applies. Listen to their testimonies, learn what they have seen,
          and hold fast to the gospel you share. At the same time, do what Paul commands for any
          teaching: "the others should weigh carefully what is said"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 14:29">1 Corinthians 14:29</a>). Kindly asking,
          "Where does Scripture say that?" is not a failure of love. It is a service to everyone.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Questions to Ask of Any Church or Teacher</h3>

        <ul>
          <li>Does it teach that salvation is by grace through faith in Christ alone, or does it add a
            required experience?</li>
          <li>Is the gospel of Christ crucified and risen at the center, or is an experience at the
            center?</li>
          <li>Do the leaders live holy lives and display the fruit of the Spirit, and not only gifts or
            results?</li>
          <li>Are prophecies and teachings tested against Scripture, as Paul instructs?</li>
          <li>Is worship orderly, loving, and aimed at building up everyone, including visitors?</li>
          <li>Are believers with different gifts, or no visible gift, honored equally as members of one
            body?</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

